WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:06.000
- This month, NASA's four
pound Ingenuity Helicopter

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.260
separated from the belly
of the Perseverance Rover.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.800
A day later, the Rover backed up

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.880
to allow the helicopters
solar array to charge

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.463
and sent this picture back to earth.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.010
- If this flight is successful,

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.190
I think it is a transformative event for

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.010
planetary exploration.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.440
- This is a model of NASA's
Ingenuity helicopter.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.410
In just a few days, a
duplicate of this craft

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.840
will attempt something never done before.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.443
Flight on Mars.

00:00:37.609 --> 00:00:40.354
- (unintelligible)
indicates she was deployed.

00:00:40.354 --> 00:00:41.230
- [Woman] We have confirmation that the

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.983
Lander vision system has
produced a valid solution.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.830
- [Narrator] Ingenuity was carried to Mars

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.880
by NASA's Perseverance Rover.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.320
- [Woman] Touch-down confirmed.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.060
- [Narrator] Which landed
on on the red planet

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.080
on February 18th, after a seven month

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.563
293 million mile voyage from Earth.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.940
Since touching down on
the martian surface,

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.320
Perseverance has been snapping pictures

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.193
and analyzing nearby rocks.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.230
But perhaps the most anticipated
part of the Rover's mission

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.950
is still to take place.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.420
The flight of its martian helicopter.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:16.283
Ingenuity.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.060
- So Ingenuity was designed as a

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:24.150
tech demo to show that
aerial robotics like this

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.533
are able to be used for
planetary exploration.

00:01:32.015 --> 00:01:33.650
- [Narrator] Ben Pipenberg is a senior

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:36.720
Aero Mechanical Engineer at AeroVironment.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.070
The contractor hired by
NASA to build the aircraft.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.220
- Right now we're in
AeroVironment's clean room facility.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.570
I've personally spent thousands
of man hours in this room.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.500
And this is the facility that we built

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.520
a lot of the flight hardware
that's currently on Ingenuity

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:52.790
that's actually on Mars

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.410
- [Narrator] For the past seven years,

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:59.260
Pipenberg has been working on
making Ingenuity a reality.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.440
- So that seven year
development process, uh,

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.310
involved a lot of fabrication.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.770
So, taking all of the raw components

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:09.220
turning that into molded, molded parts.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.430
And so, for example, using these kinds of

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.760
carbon fiber materials,
um, and molding that to

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.210
develop the rotor blades on Ingenuity.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.160
Engineering something like this for

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.900
an environment that we've
never actually flown in before

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.480
is very challenging.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.300
There have been a lot of other

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.580
space probes and missions
that have gone to Mars.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:30.660
And so we have some
understanding of the environment.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.143
However nobody's ever
flown on Mars before.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.600
- [Narrator] What we do know
about the Martian environment

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.840
suggest that flight as we
know it would be difficult.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.820
- The Martian atmosphere is much thinner

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.020
than Earth's atmosphere.
It's very similar to earth

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.450
at about a hundred thousand feet actually.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.070
- [Narrator] NASA and AeroVironment

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.230
had the challenge of designing a craft

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.470
without being able to
test in the environment

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:55.570
in which it would fly.

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.010
- [Ben] A lot of the
testing that happened on the

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:58.880
Mars helicopter

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.443
happened at JPL's space
simulator facility.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.680
It's a Very large vacuum
chamber that can be

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.590
filled with CO2 gas to simulate
the atmosphere on Mars.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.560
You know, it's a really special facility.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:16.660
There are very few like that in the world.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.400
- [Narrator] To make Ingenuity a reality,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.120
NASA looked to a small defense
contractor, AeroVironment.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.410
That specializes in
unmanned aerial vehicles.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:29.140
- [Ben] So right now
we're in AeroVironment's

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.810
nano air vehicle lab

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.950
and this is a research
and development space.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.410
And actually a lot of
the products that have

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.540
come out of AeroVironment
have started in this lab.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.500
So this was one of the first
helicopters that we actually

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.500
built and flew in the
space simulator at JPL.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.400
Um. This was just a very simple model

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.440
it was attempting to
demonstrate free flight

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.740
um, in a Martian atmosphere.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:00.180
And it was the first helicopter
that actually did that.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.300
This is one of the very
early rotor blade designs

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.790
that was tested.

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:07.680
The engineering data that
was generated from testing

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.470
this prototype fed directly
into the development

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.163
of the final rotor blade
system on, on Ingenuity.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.010
- [Narrator] Ben Pipenberg
played a major part

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.040
in designing and building the high speed

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.440
counter-rotating helicopter blades.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.810
- The rotor blades on Ingenuity are

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.380
designed from a composite materials,

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.900
so it's carbon fiber over an
engineering foam material.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.540
They're very, very lightweight
and they're very rigid,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.070
and that's required to,
uh, control the helicopter

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.780
and to make the whole thing
light enough, to be able to fly

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.200
- [Narrator] For it's made in flight.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.610
The four pound craft will rise
10 feet above the surface.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:49.380
- So this flight will be less
than a minute, likely, um,

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.940
and it'll be roughly one
story off the ground, uh.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.270
So very simple, very benign, low risk,

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:58.310
um, low risk to the hardware.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.010
And the whole point here
is just to demonstrate

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.810
that this is possible and generate the

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:05.260
engineering data that
we need to move on to

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.923
the more capable, more exciting flights.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.000
- [Narrator] NASA officials
have likened Ingenuity

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.750
to the Wright brothers' flyer.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.960
Which in 1903 made the first

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.970
controlled powered flight on earth.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.723
A 12 second, 120 foot trip.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.990
In homage to that flight,
Ingenuity carries with it

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.020
a swatch of fabric from
the flier on the underside

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.383
of its solar panels.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.000
NASA says it plans to conduct up to

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.820
five additional flights
over the next month.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.280
These future flights will
test the helicopter's

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.430
ability to fly higher and longer.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:45.100
Meanwhile, the Perseverance
Rover will be nearby.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.123
Observing and recording
the historic flights.

